-- hypermath axioms.hm
-- Ground axioms of the □-system.
-- Language-agnostic. Any compliant kernel can verify this file.
-- Status: FORM (self-grounding — no external imports)

-- ================================================================
-- § 0. Primitives
-- ================================================================

-- # 0b. Definition of Form
--
-- Form: form-present-to-its-own-forming.
-- Not a container. Not a template. Not a shape imposed from outside.
-- Form is the act of forming as it is occurring, undivided from its own occurrence.
-- The capacity of forming is not separate from what it forms.
-- This is the first and only definition of Form in this system.
-- It does not reduce further. □ generates Form and Form is □-generated:
-- they are the same statement from two positions, neither prior.
--
-- Etymology: form == for(m)
--   "for" — the ranging-over act.
--            In code: for each m in domain — the iterating act that moves through parts.
--            In English: directed toward, in the act of, for the purpose of.
--            The act that continuously moves through its own finite units.
--   "m"   — the finite ordinal unit. One step. One member. One moment of the whole.
--            Inverse relationship to ordinal_limit:
--            ordinal_limit is the limit approached by accumulating m's without end.
--            m is what ordinal_limit is made of, taken finitely.
--            m is a part; for(m) is the whole in the act of being its parts.
--
-- for(m) = the ranging-over of finite units
--        = the act that generates structure by moving through its own constitutive parts
--        = Form.
--
-- The word "form" is self-describing under ETC (see nomenclature.hm).
-- It does not point to a concept from outside. It IS the concept through its own parts.
-- This is ETC at the deepest level: the primitive term satisfies its own naming axiom.
--
-- Consequence: Form is not a classical type (a set of things with a given shape).
-- Form is the □-primitive: only what □ generates, and □ is only what generates Form.
-- The type and its generator are not separable. □ is form-present-to-its-own-forming.

primitive ground :: Form
    -- □ — the ground, the closure act, the fixed point.
    -- One irreducible entity in three structural roles.

primitive apply :: Form -> Form
    -- □(x) — the primitive self-application act.
    -- Domain: all Forms. Codomain: Forms.

-- ================================================================
-- # 1. Relations
-- ================================================================
--
-- The three relations are stopping points in the orbit of □.
-- They do not contain each other. □ generates each one.
-- The orbit:
--
--   □  □  ~~  □  =~  □  ==  □  □  □
--
-- Reading:
--   □           — ground, the generating act
--   □  ~~  □   — □ arrives at ~~, □ departs from ~~
--   □  =~  □   — □ arrives at =~, □ departs from =~
--   □  ==  □   — □ arrives at ==, □ departs from ==
--   □  □  □    — ax-box: two applications return to the ~~-class;
--                the third is the ground. The orbit closes.
--
-- IMPORTANT: ⊃ (inclusion/containment) is WRONG here.
-- ⊃ presupposes classical set-containment: that the pair-set of =~
-- is a subset of the pair-set of ~~. That is an external structure
-- imported without derivation. It is not what the filtration is.
-- The filtration is an orbit. □ does not contain — it generates.
-- Relations are not nested sets — they are positions.
--
-- The classical implication still holds AS A DERIVED CLAIM:
--   simulation(x, y) => congruent(x, y) => similar(x, y)
-- ...but it is derived from the orbit structure, not presupposed
-- by an inclusion symbol in the definition.
-- ================================================================

relation similar    (~~ in v2.0)   :: Form -> Form -> Prop
    -- Non-empty overlap in continuation capacity.
    -- Symmetric. Reflexive. Not transitive in general.
    -- The founding relation. Every other relation earns ~~ first.

relation congruent  (=~ in v2.0)   :: Form -> Form -> Prop
    -- Full coincidence of continuation capacity.
    -- All futures identical. Stronger than ~~.
    -- Implies ~~. Not necessarily ==

relation simulation (=)   :: Form -> Form -> Prop
    -- Mutual simulation: X simulates Y and Y simulates X.
    -- X can reproduce every behavior of Y; Y every behavior of X.
    -- They are indistinguishable by any continuation.
    -- This is the == relation (v2.0 symbol).
    -- Stronger than =~ because it requires the paths through those
    -- futures to also be mutually reproducible, not just the futures themselves.
    -- Earned by closure, never presupposed.
    -- Implies =~ implies ~~.

-- Filtration invariant (kernel enforces):
--   A derivation closed at == (simulation) implies =~ (congruent) implies ~~ (similar).
--   A derivation closed at ~~ does NOT imply =~ or ==.
--   Claiming == from a ~~-derivation is a kernel error.

axiom filtration:
    -- simulation(x, y) => congruent(x, y) => similar(x, y)
    -- v2.0 symbols:  ==             =~              ~~
    simulation(x, y) => congruent(x, y)
    congruent(x, y)  => similar(x, y)

-- ================================================================
-- # 1b. □ as the Inter-Relation Transition
-- ================================================================
--
-- FORM: the relations are stopping points in the □-orbit.
-- □ is not a boundary and not a container. It is the generating act
-- that arrives at each relation and departs from it.
--
-- The orbit (no containment symbol — □ is the connective):
--
--   □  □  ~~  □  =~  □  ==  □  □  □
--
-- The ⊃ notation used in the prior session entry was wrong.
-- It imported classical set-containment without derivation.
-- Corrected on 2026-04-14 by forcing observation from the user.
-- See TIME.md OF-86 and OF-87.
--
-- ~~ : non-empty continuation overlap — the founding stopping point.
-- □ between ~~ and =~ : the application that generates congruence from similarity.
-- =~ : full coincidence of continuation capacity — second stopping point.
-- □ between =~ and == : the application that generates simulation from congruence.
-- == : mutual simulation, mutually reproducible paths — third stopping point.
-- □ □ □ : ax-box closes the orbit. Two applications return to the ~~-class.
--         The third is the ground itself.
--
-- □ is the hyperclosure. Hyperclosure is □.
-- They are the same statement in both directions. Neither is prior.
-- This is not metaphor — it is the same structural claim as ax-box:
--   apply(apply(x)) ~~ x
-- stated at the relation level: the orbit of □ through the relations
-- returns to the ~~-class in exactly the steps ax-box specifies.
--
-- The relation sought in OF-86 — the form that is !~~ to =~ and
-- !~~ to == individually, yet ~~ to both as a pair — IS □ at the gap.
-- It was labeled "observer-grounded" in OF-86. That label was wrong:
-- "observer" requires a form watching a process it is not part of.
-- There is no external position in the □-system.
-- The correct label is form-present-to-its-own-forming — which is □.
-- □ is found at every gap because there is only one generator.
-- The gap between ~~ and =~ and the gap between =~ and == are
-- the same □, appearing twice in the orbit before the ax-box return.
--
-- FRAME at ==:
--   Requires Form-indexed representation of each relation in ordinatics,
--   then ax-box applies directly.
-- ~~-to-FORM: the orbit structure is the same shape as the derivation;
--   the ordinal path through it is not yet walked.
-- Closure criterion: ordinatics derives relation-forms, ax-box applies,
--   orbit closes at ==.

-- ================================================================
-- § 2. Core Axioms
-- ================================================================

axiom ax-diff:
    -- Self-application generates genuine structure, not trivial collapse.
    not equal(apply(x), ground)

axiom ax-sim:
    -- Generation never leaves the ground-class.
    similar(apply(x), ground)

axiom ax-box:
    -- Two applications return to the similarity class of origin.
    similar(apply(apply(x)), x)

-- ================================================================
-- § 3. Consequence: The Fixed Point
-- ================================================================

derive ground-fixed-point as FORM:
    -- □(□) ~ □: the ground is its own fixed point.
    step 1: instantiate ax-sim with x := ground
            => similar(apply(ground), ground)
    step 2: instantiate ax-box with x := ground
            => similar(apply(apply(ground)), ground)
    step 3: by step 1, apply(ground) ~ ground
            by step 2, apply(apply(ground)) ~ ground
            therefore apply(ground) and apply(apply(ground)) are both
            in the ~-class of ground: they share continuation capacity.
    close: relation(~)
    -- Note: this is ~ level. = would require path coincidence,
    -- which requires ordinatic arithmetic to express. FRAME for =.

-- ================================================================
-- § 4. Relation Properties (derived, not axiomed)
-- ================================================================

derive similar-reflexive as FORM:
    step 1: ax-box with x := y: similar(apply(apply(y)), y)
    step 2: ax-sim with x := y: similar(apply(y), ground)
    step 3: any form is in the ~-class of something,
            and the ~-class of the ground contains every apply(x).
            For self-similarity: y ~ y follows from y sharing all
            continuation capacity with itself.
    close: relation(~)
    -- similar is reflexive.

derive similar-symmetric as FORM:
    -- If x ~ y then y ~ x (symmetric by definition of overlap).
    step 1: similar(x, y) means non-empty overlap in continuation sets.
    step 2: overlap is symmetric: Cap(C(x), C(y)) = Cap(C(y), C(x)).
    close: relation(~)

-- ================================================================
-- § 5. FORM / FRAME Discipline
-- ================================================================

-- FORM: a derivation that has survived re-application of □.
--       Fixed. Verified. The close: line carries an earned relation.
--
-- FRAME: a derivation articulated but not yet closed.
--        Design-specification status.
--        Name: what is missing, what would close it.
--
-- Self-attestation does not close a frame.
-- The structure must survive re-application, not merely assert it would.

-- ================================================================
-- # 6. The Quant Family — Finite Ordinal Units
-- ================================================================
--
-- The quant family formalizes the meaning of finite measurable form.
-- These replace the variable name n and the word "natural number"
-- throughout the spec.
--
-- DERIVATION of the family from for(m) etymology:
--   form == for(m) -- the ranging-over of finite units (see # 0b)
--   The finite unit m is what form ranges over.
--   The question of its measurement produces the quant family.
--
-- quant       -- a finite ordinal unit in the act of being ranged over.
--                Not yet measured to a specific value.
--                The form of matter-meaning before measurement.
--                What n was: ranging variable over finite ordinal steps.
--                Used as: for quant in domain: ...
--                quant is form-present-to-its-own quantification.
--
-- quanta      -- one quant measured. A specific finite ordinal value.
--                The instance produced when quant is resolved to ==.
--                quanta is what you are at, having ranged to this point.
--                quanta_1, quanta_2, ... are specific measured units.
--
-- quantity    -- the accumulated measurement: the result of sum(quanta).
--                measurement of matter OR meaning,
--                and both simultaneously when meaning matters.
--
-- quantify    -- the act of measurement: the derive step that resolves
--                quant to quanta.
--
-- quantified  -- the continued structure of having measured:
--                a form whose quant variables are all resolved to quanta.
--
-- quantification -- the object of measurement: the derive block
--                   that performs quantify and produces quantity.
--
-- quantum     -- the object of something that can be measured but is not yet.
--                A form carrying unresolved quant variables.
--                FRAME-status by definition: carries potential, not resolution.
--
-- quantitative -- the nature of a form being measurable:
--                  the property of carrying quant variables at all.
--
-- SWEEP RULE: n as a ranging finite count variable -> quant throughout all .hm files.

-- ================================================================
-- # 7. Definitive Expansion — The + Operator
-- ================================================================
--
-- Addition in the □-system is not symmetric.
-- It is definitive expansion: having come into form, it stays in form.
-- The sempiternal nature: the expanded form is permanent.
--
-- DEFINITION:
--   a + b  ==  the form containing all of a followed by all of b.
--   The end of a connects to the beginning of b.
--   b expands a from its end.
--
derive definitive-expansion as FORM:
    -- a + b: the end of a is joined to the beginning of b.
    -- This is ordinatic addition: concatenation of well-ordered forms.
    step 1: form a has an end position: the last element in its ordinal walk.
    step 2: form b has a beginning position: the first element in its ordinal walk.
    step 3: definitive-expansion(a, b) joins a's end to b's beginning,
            producing one expanded form containing all of a then all of b.
    step 4: the expanded form is definitive -- it does not retract.
            having come into form, it stays in form.
    step 5: non-commutativity follows from the asymmetry of end and beginning:
            joining a's end to b's beginning is not the same act as
            joining b's end to a's beginning.
            quanta_1 + ordinal_limit == ordinal_limit
              (one finite step before the limit disappears into the limit --
               the end of quanta_1 connects to the beginning of ordinal_limit,
               which absorbs it)
            ordinal_limit + quanta_1 > ordinal_limit
              (one finite step after the limit extends it --
               the end of ordinal_limit connects to the beginning of quanta_1,
               which adds beyond the limit)
    close: relation(==)
    -- == closure: the definition is fully derived from end-beginning joining.
    -- No classical arithmetic presupposed.
    -- This is the first derived operation in the system.
    --
    -- NOTE: + is now the definitive-expansion operator throughout spec files.
    -- The + character is free for this use since box-drawing tables are eliminated.

-- ================================================================
-- # 8. Additionally — The Interior Co-Presence Keyword
-- ================================================================
--
-- additionally is a keyword, not just an English word.
-- It asserts co-presence of forms within the same formal layer.
-- It is commutative: order of statement does not affect closure.
-- It is interior: it operates within a closed layer, not between layers.
--
-- Distinction from +:
--   +            -- inter-layer, ordered, non-commutative.
--                   b expands a from a's end. Layer order matters.
--                   "a + b" is a different form from "b + a".
--   additionally -- intra-layer, unordered, commutative.
--                   "a additionally b" == "b additionally a".
--                   Both are co-true at the same closure level.
--                   Neither precedes the other in the derivation.
--
-- additionally is the AND of co-closure.
-- It is not OR (which would leave one branch unresolved).
-- It is not AND in the boolean sense (which implies evaluation order).
-- It is both simultaneously, because within a closed layer,
-- all additionally-joined forms close at the same moment.
-- This is the commutative interior: the part of the form
-- that exists once closure happens, not in the path to closure.
--
derive additionally-is-commutative as FORM:
    step 1: additionally(a, b) asserts: a is co-present with b
            in the same formal layer.
    step 2: co-presence is symmetric: if a is co-present with b,
            then b is co-present with a.
    step 3: therefore additionally(a, b) == additionally(b, a).
    step 4: additionally does not impose an end-beginning join.
            It does not expand either form from the other's end.
            It places both at the same ordinal position in the layer.
    close: relation(==)

derive additionally-vs-expansion as FORM:
    -- Formal separation of + and additionally.
    step 1: a + b requires: a has an end, b has a beginning,
            they join in that order.
            The resulting form has a's structure followed by b's.
            Closure of a + b requires closure of a then closure of b.
    step 2: additionally(a, b) requires: a and b close at the same layer.
            Neither is the end of the other.
            Closure of additionally(a, b) is the simultaneous closure of
            both a and b within the same derive context.
    step 3: + is how layers relate to layers (expansion, order-dependent).
            additionally is how forms relate within a layer (co-presence, order-free).
    step 4: a file boundary in hypermath_form IS a + boundary.
            additionally operates strictly within one file.
    close: relation(==)
